<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
        Self-hosted media servers frequently use HTTP on a trusted LAN. The UI surfaces a warning
        for these profiles; HTTPS remains recommended.

        This deliberately stays a blanket permission, and `InsecureBaseConfiguration` is suppressed
        rather than worked around. A <domain-config> cannot express the rule Spole actually
        enforces: domain matching here is a right-anchored hostname suffix, the same way DNS works,
        so "192.168" does not match "192.168.1.20" and there is no CIDR syntax to reach for. Only
        an exhaustive list of literal addresses would work, and the whole point is that the user's
        server can be at any address on their own network.

        The real check is `EndpointValidator.isTrustedLanHost`, which accepts http:// only for
        localhost, a .local name, or a literal RFC 1918 / loopback / link-local address, and which
        deliberately refuses registered names that merely look private. Every address the app
        connects to passes through `normalizeBaseUrl` first, so that check is not advisory.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration" />
</network-security-config>
